{#
	The stat bar, shared by the home page and every category page.

	A macro rather than a copy in each: the two show the same figures over
	different sets, and a second copy is how one of them ends up a release
	behind. `stats` is whatever set is being summarised — `sites.stats` for the
	whole fleet, `group.stats` for one category.

	Everything here degrades on its own: the weight sparkline only exists for the
	whole fleet, the Eleventy share only appears when there is one, and the Core
	Web Vitals tile only appears where CrUX has sampled anything. So a category
	page shows fewer tiles than the home page without the caller deciding which.
#}
{% macro statbar(stats, metrics) %}
<section class="statbar">
	{#
		Six medians, each computed on its own column — so this is a summary of the
		fleet rather than a portrait of any one site, and no site necessarily has
		this exact row.

		The Core Web Vitals ring rests on far fewer sites than the other five,
		since most of this corpus is too small for CrUX to sample. Its own tooltip
		says how many rather than letting the row imply they are all equally
		grounded.
	#}
	{#
		Both rows, because they disagree and the disagreement is the point: the
		median says what the middle site scores, the mean is dragged by a long
		tail. Across the whole fleet that is 98 against 90 for performance, and 2
		against 13 for axe violations — one row alone would be a different claim.
	#}
	<div class="stat stat-rings">
		<div class="ring-row">
			<span class="rings">
				{% for score in metrics.SCORES %}
				{% scoreRing stats.medians.scores[score.key], "Median " + score.label %}
				{% endfor %}
				{% axeRing stats.medians.axe, 37, "Median axe violations: " + (stats.medians.axe | default("no data")) + ", across " + (stats.medians.counts.axe | num) + " sites" %}
				{% cwvRing stats.medians.cwvFailures, null, 37, "Median Core Web Vitals: " + (stats.medians.cwvFailures | default("no data")) + " failing, across " + (stats.medians.counts.cwv | num) + " sites with real-user data" %}
			</span>
			<span class="stat-label">Median scores</span>
		</div>
		<div class="ring-row">
			<span class="rings">
				{% for score in metrics.SCORES %}
				{% scoreRing stats.means.scores[score.key], "Mean " + score.label %}
				{% endfor %}
				{% axeRing stats.means.axe, 37, "Mean axe violations: " + (stats.means.axe | default("no data")) + " per site, rounded, across " + (stats.means.counts.axe | num) + " sites" %}
				{% cwvRing stats.means.cwvFailures, null, 37, "Mean Core Web Vitals: " + (stats.means.cwvFailures | default("no data")) + " failing per site, rounded, across " + (stats.means.counts.cwv | num) + " sites with real-user data" %}
			</span>
			<span class="stat-label">Mean scores</span>
		</div>
		<p class="stat-sub-note muted small">
			Across {{ stats.medians.counts.scores | num }} sites.
			<span title="Only sites CrUX has real-user data for can have a Core Web Vitals figure at all">Core Web Vitals across {{ stats.medians.counts.cwv | num }} sites.</span>
		</p>
	</div>
	<div class="stat" title="Sites scoring 100 in all four Lighthouse categories, with no axe violations and no Core Web Vital failing for real users">
		<span class="stat-label">Perfect {{ stats.perfect | plural("score") }}</span>
		<span class="stat-value">{{ stats.perfect }}<span class="stat-suffix">/{{ stats.measured }}</span></span>
		{#
			What built them, rather than one figure for Eleventy and nothing about
			the rest. The rows sum to the number above — a site with no generator
			tag is counted under its own row instead of being dropped, which is
			what would make the two disagree.

			A presumption counts the same as a detection, as it does everywhere
			else on this site: most static sites emit no generator tag, so
			detections alone would credit the wrong thing.
		#}
		{% if stats.perfectGenerators.length %}
		{#
			`stat-tally` rather than `tally`: that class is the Technology section's
			card, with its own border and padding, and reusing the name here drew a
			box around this table and would have reformatted that one.
		#}
		<table class="stat-tally">
			{% for g in stats.perfectGenerators %}
			<tr>
				<th scope="row">
					{% for mark in g.marks %}{% stackIcon mark, 14 %}{% endfor %}
					{#- No generator at all is the absence of a value, not a brand
					    called "None" — italics say so without a second column. #}
					<span class="stat-tally-name">{% if g.id %}{{ g.name }}{% else %}<em>{{ g.name }}</em>{% endif %}</span>
				</th>
				<td title="{{ g.count | num }} of the {{ stats.perfect | num }} perfect {{ stats.perfect | plural('score') }} ({{ (g.count / stats.perfect * 100) | round }}%)">{{ g.count | num }}</td>
			</tr>
			{% endfor %}
		</table>
		{% endif %}
	</div>
	<div class="stat">
		{#
			A distribution rather than an average. The mean here is 1.11 MB and the
			median 256 kB — four times apart, because a handful of sites in the tens
			of megabytes drag the average somewhere almost no site actually sits. A
			single number could not say that; the shape does, at a glance.
		#}
		<span class="stat-label">Page weight</span>
		{% set weightMax = stats.weightBuckets | maxValue("count") %}
		<span class="histogram" role="img"
			aria-label="Page weight distribution across {{ stats.measured | num }} sites{% for b in stats.weightBuckets %}. {{ b.label }}: {{ b.count | num }}{% endfor %}">
			{% for b in stats.weightBuckets %}
			<span class="histogram-col" title="{{ b.label }} — {{ b.count | num }} {{ b.count | plural("site") }} ({{ b.share }}%)">
				<span class="histogram-track">
					<span class="histogram-bar" style="height:{{ (b.count / weightMax * 100) | round(1) }}%"></span>
				</span>
				<span class="histogram-tick">{{ b.tick }}</span>
			</span>
			{% endfor %}
		</span>
		<p class="stat-sub-note muted small">
			Median <strong>{{ stats.medianWeight | bytes }}</strong> · mean {{ stats.avgWeight | bytes }}
		</p>
		{#
			JavaScript on its own, under the total. Its own bucket edges, because it
			is an order of magnitude smaller than the page around it — see
			SCRIPT_BUCKETS in lib/report.js — and its own leading bucket for the
			sites that ship none at all.
		#}
		{% if stats.scriptBuckets.length %}
		{% set scriptMax = stats.scriptBuckets | maxValue("count") %}
		<span class="stat-label stat-label-sub">JavaScript Weight</span>
		<span class="histogram" role="img"
			aria-label="JavaScript weight distribution across {{ stats.measured | num }} sites{% for b in stats.scriptBuckets %}. {{ b.label }}: {{ b.count | num }}{% endfor %}">
			{% for b in stats.scriptBuckets %}
			<span class="histogram-col" title="{{ b.label }} — {{ b.count | num }} {{ b.count | plural("site") }} ({{ b.share }}%)">
				<span class="histogram-track">
					<span class="histogram-bar histogram-bar-script" style="height:{{ (b.count / scriptMax * 100) | round(1) }}%"></span>
				</span>
				<span class="histogram-tick">{{ b.tick }}</span>
			</span>
			{% endfor %}
		</span>
		<p class="stat-sub-note muted small">
			Median <strong>{{ stats.medianScript | bytes }}</strong> ·
			{{ stats.scriptBuckets[0].count | num }} ship none
		</p>
		{% endif %}
		{#
			Absent until the report has enough days to be worth drawing — see
			MIN_WEIGHT_HISTORY_DAYS in lib/report.js.
		#}
		{% if stats.weightHistory.length %}
		{% set weightTrend = { values: [] } %}
		{% for point in stats.weightHistory %}
			{% set weightTrend = (weightTrend.values.push(point.avgWeight), weightTrend) %}
		{% endfor %}
		<span class="stat-spark" title="Daily average across every site measured that day, {{ stats.weightHistory[0].date }} to {{ stats.weightHistory[stats.weightHistory.length - 1].date }}">
			{% sparkline weightTrend, { width: 150, height: 24 } %}
		</span>
		{% endif %}
	</div>
	{% if stats.recentlyMeasured.length %}
	<div class="stat">
		{#
			What the measurement loop has just done. Everything else on this bar is
			a state of the corpus; this is the only thing that shows it moving.
		#}
		<span class="stat-label">Recently measured</span>
		<ol class="recents">
			{% for site in stats.recentlyMeasured %}
			<li>
				<a href="/site/{{ site.slug }}/">{{ site.displayUrl }}</a>
				<span class="recents-age">{{ site.timestamp | since }}</span>
			</li>
			{% endfor %}
		</ol>
	</div>
	{% endif %}
	{% if stats.measured %}
	<div class="stat">
		{#
			Three states rather than a fraction. "890 of 1,441 passing" implied the
			other 551 were failing, when almost all of them have no real-user data
			at all — CrUX only reports sites with enough Chrome traffic, which most
			of this corpus does not have. Unknown is the majority answer here, and
			hiding it inside a denominator overstated both of the other two.
		#}
		<span class="stat-label">Core Web Vitals</span>
		<table class="verdicts">
			<tr class="verdict verdict-good">
				<th scope="row">Passing</th>
				<td title="Every Core Web Vital CrUX reports for this site is good at p75">{{ stats.cwvVerdicts.passing | num }}</td>
			</tr>
			<tr class="verdict verdict-poor">
				<th scope="row">Failing</th>
				<td title="At least one Core Web Vital is not good at p75">{{ stats.cwvVerdicts.failing | num }}</td>
			</tr>
			<tr class="verdict verdict-none">
				<th scope="row">Unknown</th>
				<td title="Too little Chrome traffic for CrUX to report on — no real-user verdict either way">{{ stats.cwvVerdicts.unknown | num }}</td>
			</tr>
		</table>
	</div>
	{% endif %}
</section>
{% endmacro %}
